6w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14198.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14198.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14198.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14198.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14198.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14198.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14198.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14198.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14198.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14198.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>89th of the 108 Parshwanaths - Shri Kareda Parshwanath</p><p>Main Deity: The tall image of Lord Shri Kareda Parshwanath is black in color, seated in the lotus (padmasana) posture, 29 inches high and 27 inches wide, with a canopy of 9 serpent hoods above its head.</p><p>Location: This temple stands in the center of the town of Bhupalsagar.</p><p>History: No firm evidence of the antiquity of this pilgrimage site is available. During the recent renovation (jinoddhar), an ancient pillar was found bearing an inscription of Samvat 55, suggesting that this pilgrimage site may date to before the Vikram era. In Vikram Samvat 861, Shri Khimsingh Shah had a magnificent shrine built here. The image of Lord Parshwanath was consecrated by the auspicious hands of Acharya Shri Jayanand Surishwarji. There is also mention of a consecration of the image of Shri Parshwanath Bhagwan performed by Bhattarak Acharya Shri Yashobhadrasurishwarji in Vikram Samvat 1039. It is recorded that on Chaitra Krishna Somvati Amavasya of Samvat 1326, Maharawal Shri Chachigdev donated funds for the service and worship of the temple of Shri Parshwanath Bhagwan here. Inscriptions from Vikram Samvat 1303, 1341, and 1496 are carved on some images in the bhamti (circumambulatory passage). An image resembling a mosque had been built atop the assembly hall (sabha mandap); it is said that when Emperor Akbar came here, this feature had been built so that Muslim invaders would not destroy this beautiful, miraculous temple. According to the Gurvavali, the great minister Shri Pethadshah of Mandavgadh also had a temple of Shri Parshwanath Bhagwan built here. It is recorded that in Vikram Samvat 1340, when Shri Jhanjhan Shah, son of the great minister Shri Pethadshah of Mandavgadh, led a chhari-palit pilgrimage sangh to Shri Shatrunjaygiri accompanied by 20 acharya bhagwants and many monks and nuns, he came here and began the renovation of the temple of Shri Parshwaprabhu, having a seven-storied temple built — though today that magnificent temple can no longer be found. The present temple is believed to have been built in Samvat 1039. An inscription of Vikram Samvat 1656 is carved on the image of the Lord; it is possible that this image was consecrated at the time of a renovation. This temple was recently renovated again, and the re-consecration of this ancient image of the Lord took place on the auspicious 13th day of the bright half of Magh in Vikram Samvat 2033, performed by the auspicious hands of Acharya Shri Sushilsurishwarji. This temple, complete with 52 subsidiary shrines (bavan jinalay), is extremely beautiful, and is believed to have been consecrated by the scholarly Bhattarak Acharya Shri Yashobhadrasurishwarji. There is a record of Emperor Akbar&#x27;s visit here for darshan. Mantri Jhanjhan Shah, son of the great minister of Mandavgadh Shri Pethadishah, came here with Acharya Shri Dharmghoshsurishwarji and many other acharyas as part of a Shatrunjaya pilgrimage sangh notable in Jain history, to have darshan of this image of Shri Parshwaprabhu, remover of afflictions — it was here that the sanghpati&#x27;s tilak (mark of honor) was conferred. The present image, too, is highly miraculous and removes afflictions. Every year on the Lord&#x27;s birth-anniversary (janma kalyanak), the 10th day of the dark half of Paush, a fair is held here, in which thousands of men and women take part in devotion to the Lord. On that day, the rays of the sun fall directly upon the image.</p><p>Five Consecrations at Once: Acharya Shri Yashobhadrasurishwarji of the Sanderak gachchha was an extremely powerful and great personage, and many miraculous events occurred in his life. An account of a miraculous event that took place at the consecration of Shri Kareda Tirth is found in the text &quot;Prabandh Panchashati.&quot; While the revered Acharya Bhagwant was residing in Aghatpur, magnificent Jina temples had just been completed in the towns of Karhetpur, Kavilanpur, Sanyabharipur, Mandorpur, and Bhaisanpur. The sanghs of these five towns approached the Acharya Bhagwant to obtain an auspicious moment (muhurat) for the consecration of their respective temples. The Acharya Bhagwant granted a single muhurat for the consecration of all five temples, and, to the astonishment of all, the revered Suridev assumed four additional forms and performed the consecration of all five Jina temples at that very same auspicious moment.</p><p>Other Temples: At present there are no other temples.</p><p>Art and Sculpture: Many ruins can be seen here offering a glimpse of ancient art. If proper research were undertaken, much authentic historical detail could likely be uncovered. A distinctive feature of this temple is that the Mulanayak image has a double spire-flag (dhwaja) above it.</p><p>Directions: The nearest railway station to Bhupalsagar is 1 km away. This place lies on the Chittor-Udaipur road, 56 km from Chittor. Bus service and private vehicles are available. Dharmashala and bhojanshala facilities are available here.</p><p>Scripture References: This temple is mentioned in the &quot;Tirthamala,&quot; the &quot;Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,&quot; the &quot;Shri Bhatev Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; the &quot;Shri Godiji Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; the &quot;Shri Parshwanath Nammala,&quot; the &quot;Shri Parshwanath Chaityaparipati,&quot; and others. The &quot;Prabandh Panchashati,&quot; written by Shri Shubhshil Gani in Vikram Samvat 1521, records the miracles that occurred during the installation of the image. An image of Shri Kareda Parshwanath is also found in the Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Santacruz, Mumbai, at the 108 Parshwanath Bhakti Vihar Tirth, and at Jiravala Tirth.</p><p>Praise (Stuti): O Lord, whose renovation was carried out by minister Jhanjhan — through praise of you, all divinely-caused afflictions are driven far away; your name is such that it strikes karma like a whip. &quot;With devotion I bow to Shri Kareda Parshwa.&quot;</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hrim Arham Shri Kareda Parshwanathaya Namah.&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Jain Shwetambar Murtipujak Shri Kareda Parshwanathji Tirth Pedhi, Bhupalsagar: 312052, District: Chittorgarh, State of Rajasthan, India. Phone: 01476-84233, Mobile: 07073224862.</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between